/* ========== GCSE · LEVEL 5 · Grade 9 ========== */
var GP9=['g_heattime','g_effchain','g_impulse','g_emwave','g_brake','g_kegpe','g_heat','g_lift','g_cost','g_suvat','g_trans','g_boyle'],GC9=['g_yieldmass','g_gasreact','g_titr','g_conc2','g_molebal','g_limit','g_bond','g_atomecon'],GM9=['g_quadineq','g_simulquad','g_surdrat','g_bounds2','g_functrans','g_circeq','g_iter','g_qnth','g_segment','g_pythag3d','g_reversepct','g_recur'];
U({id:'gp5-1',track:'gp',level:5,title:'Unfamiliar contexts',icon:'⚛︎',color:'#7F77DD',desc:'Grade 9 questions put familiar physics into situations you\'ve never seen, like a nuclear power station or a hospital.',lessons:[
LH('gp5-1-1','Physics in a nuclear power station',11,'Specific heat capacity, efficiency, half-life and radiation, applied to a real reactor like Hinkley Point C.',[
T(`<h3>Don't panic at a new context</h3><p>Grade 9 questions often describe something you've never studied. The physics underneath is always on the specification. Strip the context away: what quantities are given, and which equation links them?</p>`),
T(`<h3>Heat from the core</h3><p>In a pressurised water reactor, water flows through the core and is heated from about 290 °C to 325 °C. It stays liquid because it's at very high pressure. Hot, high-pressure water has c ≈ 5500 J/kg °C.</p>`),
N(`How much energy does each kilogram of coolant carry away from the core? (c = 5500 J/kg °C, temperature rise 290 °C → 325 °C)`,192500,0.005,'J',`ΔE = mcΔθ = 1 × 5500 × 35 = 192 500 J per kg.`),
N(`About 18 000 kg of coolant flows through the core every second. Using your last answer, what is the thermal power in megawatts? (3 significant figures)`,3470,0.01,'MW',`18 000 × 192 500 = 3.465 × 10⁹ J/s ≈ 3470 MW. Power is energy per second.`),
N(`A reactor produces 4500 MW of heat and 1600 MW of electricity. What is its efficiency, as a percentage? (1 decimal place)`,35.6,0.003,'%',`1600 ÷ 4500 × 100 = 35.6%. The rest is given out to the sea through the condensers.`),
Q(`Why is so much energy (over 60%) wasted?`,[`Turning heat into work always wastes some energy as heat to the surroundings, here the cooling water`,'The reactor leaks heat through its thick concrete walls, so most of it never reaches the turbine hall','The generator is badly designed, so most of the electricity is lost as sound','Energy is destroyed in the turbine, because spinning it uses up the energy'],0,`Energy isn't destroyed; most is transferred to the sea by the cooling water.`),
N(`Iodine-131, a fission product, has a half-life of 8 days. What fraction is left after 32 days? Give the bottom number of 1/?`,16,0.001,'',`32 ÷ 8 = 4 half-lives. (½)⁴ = 1/16.`),
Q(`Why is the reactor surrounded by thick concrete?`,[`To absorb gamma radiation and neutrons`,'To keep the reactor warm and steady','To stop the alpha particles only','To hold in the water if it leaks'],0,`Gamma needs thick dense shielding; alpha is stopped by almost anything.`),
T(`<h3>Key points</h3><ul><li>Strip the context back to the quantities and the equation.</li><li>Power = energy per second, so multiply energy per kg by kg per second.</li><li>Wasted energy goes to the surroundings, not nowhere.</li></ul>`)]),
LH('gp5-1-2','New situations, familiar physics',11,'Medicine, transport and space: applying the specification to contexts you\'ve not met.',[
RM(`<p>Find the quantities given, then the equation that links them. The context is just decoration.</p>`),
Q(`A PET scanner uses a tracer that emits positrons, which quickly produce gamma rays. Why must the tracer have a short half-life?`,[`So the patient isn't exposed to radiation for long`,'So it\'s cheaper and quicker to make in the hospital','So it travels faster around the body','So it emits alpha particles as well as gamma rays'],0,`The same reasoning as any medical tracer.`),
Q(`A train uses regenerative braking: the motors work as generators while slowing down. What energy transfer happens?`,[`Kinetic energy is transferred electrically back to the supply`,'Chemical energy is transferred to the kinetic store','Thermal energy in the brakes becomes kinetic energy','Kinetic energy is transferred to the thermal store only'],0,`Less energy is wasted heating the brakes.`),
Q(`An astronaut on the Moon (g = 1.6 N/kg) drops a hammer and a feather together. What happens?`,[`They land at the same time, because there's no air resistance`,'The hammer lands first, because it has a bigger weight','The feather floats, because the Moon\'s gravity is so weak','The feather lands first, because it has less mass to move'],0,`With no air, all objects have the same acceleration.`),
N(`A 70 kg astronaut climbs 4 m up a ladder on the Moon (g = 1.6 N/kg). How much gravitational potential energy does she gain?`,448,0.005,'J',`E<sub>p</sub> = mgh = 70 × 1.6 × 4 = 448 J.`),
Q(`Electric cars are often described as "zero emission". Why might a physicist disagree?`,[`The electricity may come from power stations that burn fossil fuels`,'They give out CO₂ from the exhaust pipe whenever the battery is running low','Their batteries give out radiation when they are charging','They use a small amount of petrol to start the motor'],0,`Emissions move to the power station, unless it's low-carbon (like nuclear or wind).`),
T(`<h3>Key points</h3><ul><li>Medical sources: choose type and half-life for the job.</li><li>No air resistance: everything falls with the same acceleration.</li><li>Evaluate claims by following the energy back to its source.</li></ul>`)])
]});
U({id:'gp5-2',track:'gp',level:5,title:'Multi-step problems',icon:'⇶',color:'#D85A30',desc:'The hardest calculations: several equations, conversions and rearranging in one question.',lessons:[
LH('gp5-2-1','Chaining equations',12,'Three-step problems, with conversions, where you have to plan the route.',[
T(`<h3>Plan the route first</h3><p>Write the target quantity at the bottom of the page and the given quantities at the top. Find a chain of equations connecting them. Only then put numbers in.</p>`),
W('stepCalc',{steps:[`A 1200 kg car at 25 m/s brakes to rest in 4.0 s. Find the average braking force and the braking distance.`,`Momentum change: 1200 × 25 = 30 000 kg m/s`,`Force = change in momentum ÷ time = 30 000 ÷ 4.0 = <b>7500 N</b>`,`Deceleration a = F ÷ m = 7500 ÷ 1200 = 6.25 m/s²`,`v² − u² = 2as: 0 − 625 = 2 × (−6.25) × s, so <b>s = 50 m</b>`]},`A grade 9 worked example.`,`One answer feeds the next.`),
W('calcDrill',{skill:GP9,need:8},`Grade 9 mixed calculations. Get 8 right to master it.`,`Plan the chain before calculating.`),
T(`<h3>Key points</h3><ul><li>Target at the bottom, givens at the top, then join them.</li><li>Keep full values until the end.</li></ul>`)]),
LH('gp5-2-2','Standard form and significant figures under pressure',11,'Exam-style answers with very big or small numbers, and rounding correctly.',[
RM(`<p>Standard form: A × 10ⁿ. Give answers to the same significant figures as the data.</p>`),
N(`A radio station broadcasts at 97.0 MHz. What is the wavelength? (v = 3.00 × 10⁸ m/s, 3 significant figures)`,3.09,0.005,'m',`λ = 3.00 × 10⁸ ÷ 97.0 × 10⁶ = 3.09 m.`),
N(`The Sun's power output is 3.8 × 10²⁶ W. How much energy does it give out in one day? Give the number in front of × 10³¹ J.`,3.28,0.01,'× 10³¹ J',`E = Pt = 3.8 × 10²⁶ × 86 400 = 3.28 × 10³¹ J.`),
N(`A nucleus has a radius of about 1.2 × 10⁻¹⁵ m and an atom 1.0 × 10⁻¹⁰ m. How many times bigger is the atom? Give the number in front of × 10⁴.`,8.3,0.02,'× 10⁴',`1.0 × 10⁻¹⁰ ÷ 1.2 × 10⁻¹⁵ = 8.3 × 10⁴ times.`),
W('calcDrill',{skill:['g_emwave','g_stdform','g_sigfig','g_impulse'],need:6},`Big and small numbers. Get 6 right to master it.`,`Use the ×10ˣ button and round at the end.`),
T(`<h3>Key points</h3><ul><li>Use your calculator's ×10ˣ button, not "× 10 ^".</li><li>Round to the s.f. of the least precise data.</li></ul>`)])
]});
U({id:'gp5-3',track:'gp',level:5,title:'Data and graphs',icon:'📉',color:'#1D9E75',desc:'Tangents to curves, areas under graphs, uncertainty, and evaluating data and conclusions.',lessons:[
LH('gp5-3-1','Tangents and areas',11,'Finding the gradient of a curve at a point, and estimating areas under curved graphs.',[
T(`<h3>Gradient of a curve</h3><p>A curve's gradient changes. To find it at one point, draw a <b>tangent</b>: a straight line that just touches the curve there. Then find the gradient of the tangent using two points far apart on it.</p><p>On a distance–time graph, the tangent's gradient is the speed at that instant.</p>`),
W('fnPlot',{x:[0,10],y:[0,100],xl:'time (s)',yl:'distance (m)',sl:[['t','Time of tangent',1,9,1,5,function(v){return v+' s'}]],curves:function(p){var f=function(x){return x*x},m=2*p.t,c=p.t*p.t-m*p.t;return [[f,'--accent',3],[function(x){return m*x+c},'--muted',2,[6,4]]]},read:function(p){return 'At t = '+p.t+' s, the tangent has gradient <b>'+(2*p.t)+' m/s</b>: that\'s the speed at that moment. The curve gets steeper, so the object is accelerating.'}},`Move the tangent along the curve d = t².`,`The gradient of the tangent is the instantaneous speed.`),
Q(`On a velocity–time graph that curves upward, how do you find the acceleration at a point?`,[`Draw a tangent there and find its gradient`,`Find the area under the curve`,`Read the y-value`,`Divide velocity by time from the origin`],0,`Gradient of v–t = acceleration.`),
T(`<h3>Area under a curve</h3><p>For a curved velocity–time graph, estimate the distance by counting squares (each square is worth so many metres) or by splitting it into trapeziums.</p>`),
N(`On a v–t graph, each grid square is 2 m/s by 1 s. The area under the curve is about 37 squares. Roughly how far did it travel?`,74,0.01,'m',`Each square = 2 × 1 = 2 m. 37 × 2 = 74 m.`),
T(`<h3>Key points</h3><ul><li>Tangent gradient = rate at that instant.</li><li>Area under v–t = distance; count squares and multiply by each square's value.</li></ul>`)]),
LH('gp5-3-2','Evaluating data and conclusions',10,'Uncertainty, valid conclusions, and what you can and can\'t claim from data.',[
RM(`<p>Precise: readings close together. Accurate: close to the true value. Random and systematic errors.</p>`),
T(`<h3>Uncertainty</h3><p>The <b>uncertainty</b> in a set of repeat readings is about <b>half the range</b>: (largest − smallest) ÷ 2. Write the answer as mean ± uncertainty.</p>`),
N(`Repeat readings: 2.4 s, 2.6 s, 2.5 s, 2.7 s. What is the uncertainty (± seconds)?`,0.15,0.01,'s',`Range = 2.7 − 2.4 = 0.3 s. Half the range = ± 0.15 s.`),
T(`<h3>Valid conclusions</h3><ul><li>A conclusion should only describe what the data shows, within the range tested.</li><li>A correlation doesn't prove one thing causes another.</li><li>A <b>valid</b> experiment answers the question it set out to answer, with control variables kept the same.</li></ul>`),
Q(`A student finds resistance increases with wire length for lengths 10–50 cm. Which conclusion is valid?`,[`Between 10 and 50 cm, resistance increases with length`,`Resistance always increases with length, at any length`,`Length causes all resistance`,`Thicker wires have more resistance`],0,`Don't extrapolate beyond the data.`),
Q(`Two groups get different results with the same method. Their results are not…`,[`reproducible`,`accurate`,`random`,`valid by definition`],0,`Reproducible means others get the same results.`),
T(`<h3>Key points</h3><ul><li>Uncertainty ≈ half the range.</li><li>Conclusions only within the range tested.</li><li>Correlation isn't causation.</li></ul>`)])
]});
U({id:'gp5-4',track:'gp',level:5,title:'Full-marks technique',icon:'✓',color:'#BA7517',desc:'Command words, and the small mistakes that cost grade 9 candidates marks.',lessons:[
LH('gp5-4-1','Command words',9,'Exactly what each command word is asking for.',[
W('matchPairs',{pairs:[['State / Give','A short answer, no explanation'],['Describe','Say what happens, step by step'],['Explain','Say why: give reasons with "because"'],['Compare','Similarities and differences, both sides in each sentence'],['Evaluate','Pros and cons, then a justified judgement'],['Suggest','Apply your knowledge to an unfamiliar situation']]},`Match each command word to what it asks for.`,`Answer the command word, not the topic.`),
Q(`"Explain why the current in a filament lamp doesn't increase in proportion to the p.d." What must your answer include?`,[`A reason: the filament heats up, so its resistance increases`,'A description: the graph is a curve, not a straight line','A labelled circuit diagram of the lamp and the meters','A calculation of the resistance at two different p.d.s'],0,`Explain = why.`),
Q(`"Compare alpha and gamma radiation." Which is a good comparison sentence?`,[`Alpha is strongly ionising, whereas gamma is weakly ionising`,'Alpha is strongly ionising and gamma is a type of radiation','Gamma is an electromagnetic wave, and alpha is a particle','Both are ionising radiation given out by unstable nuclei'],0,`Mention both in the same sentence.`),
T(`<h3>Key points</h3><ul><li>State: short. Describe: what. Explain: why.</li><li>Compare: both sides each time ("whereas").</li><li>Evaluate: finish with a judgement.</li></ul>`)]),
LH('gp5-4-2','How marks are lost',10,'The small errors that cost top candidates marks, and how to avoid them.',[
RM(`<p>Write the equation, convert, substitute, calculate, give the unit.</p>`),
T(`<h3>The usual suspects</h3><ul><li>Forgetting to convert cm → m, g → kg, minutes → s, kW → W.</li><li>Missing or wrong units.</li><li>Squaring at the wrong time (½mv²: square v only).</li><li>Rounding too early.</li><li>Using 10 instead of 9.8 N/kg.</li><li>Writing "energy is lost" instead of "energy is transferred to the surroundings".</li><li>Saying "particles get bigger" when heated (they move faster).</li></ul>`),
Q(`A student writes "the energy is lost as heat". How should a grade 9 answer say it?`,[`Energy is transferred to the thermal store of the surroundings`,'Energy is destroyed as heat leaves the system and the surroundings','The energy disappears into the air as heat','Heat energy is lost from the system forever'],0,`Energy is conserved, so be precise.`),
Q(`What's wrong with "the particles expand when heated"?`,[`The particles stay the same size; they move faster and further apart`,'Nothing: particles do get bigger when they are heated','The particles shrink when heated, so there\'s more space','Only gas particles expand when heated; solid and liquid particles don\'t'],0,`A very common lost mark.`),
Q(`E = ½mv² with m = 2 kg, v = 3 m/s. Which is right?`,[`9 J`,`36 J`,`18 J`,`3 J`],0,`0.5 × 2 × 3² = 9. Squaring (½ × 2 × 3) gives 9 by luck here, but usually it's wrong.`),
T(`<h3>Key points</h3><ul><li>Convert, include units, and round only at the end.</li><li>Energy is transferred, never lost.</li><li>Particles don't change size.</li></ul>`)])
]});

U({id:'gc5-1',track:'gc',level:5,title:'Hardest calculations',icon:'∑',color:'#BA7517',desc:'Multi-step mole calculations, and using yield and atom economy to choose between routes.',lessons:[
LH('gc5-1-1','Multi-step mole problems',12,'Mass, solutions and gases in one question.',[
T(`<h3>Every route goes through moles</h3><div class="eq">mass ⇄ moles ⇄ concentration × volume ⇄ gas volume</div><p>Use the equation's ratio to jump from one substance's moles to another's.</p>`),
W('stepCalc',{steps:[`2.50 g of CaCO₃ reacts with excess HCl. What volume of CO₂ forms, and what volume of 2.0 mol/dm³ HCl is needed? (CaCO₃ = 100)`,`Moles of CaCO₃ = 2.50 ÷ 100 = 0.0250 mol`,`CaCO₃ + 2HCl → CaCl₂ + H₂O + CO₂: 0.0250 mol CO₂ → 0.0250 × 24 = <b>0.600 dm³</b>`,`HCl needed = 2 × 0.0250 = 0.0500 mol`,`Volume = 0.0500 ÷ 2.0 = 0.0250 dm³ = <b>25.0 cm³</b>`]},`A grade 9 worked example.`,`One set of moles, three different answers.`),
W('calcDrill',{skill:GC9,need:8},`Grade 9 mole problems. Get 8 right to master it.`,`Moles first, every time.`),
T(`<h3>Key points</h3><ul><li>Mass, solution and gas all convert through moles.</li><li>Use the ratio to switch substances.</li></ul>`)]),
LH('gc5-1-2','Choosing a route',11,'Using yield, atom economy, rate, equilibrium and cost to decide which reaction an industry should use.',[
RM(`<p>Percentage yield = actual ÷ theoretical × 100. Atom economy = useful M<sub>r</sub> ÷ total reactant M<sub>r</sub> × 100.</p>`),
T(`<h3>Comparing routes</h3><p>Ethanol can be made two ways:</p><ul><li><b>Fermentation</b>: C₆H₁₂O₆ → 2C₂H₅OH + 2CO₂. Atom economy 51%, renewable sugar, slow, impure product, low temperature.</li><li><b>Hydration of ethene</b>: C₂H₄ + H₂O → C₂H₅OH. Atom economy 100%, fast, pure, but ethene comes from crude oil and needs 300 °C and 60–70 atm.</li></ul>`),
Q(`Which is the best argument for fermentation?`,[`It uses a renewable raw material`,`It has a 100% atom economy`,'It\'s a much faster process than hydration','It gives purer ethanol than any other method'],0,`Evaluate: renewable vs efficient.`),
W('calcDrill',{skill:['g_atomecon','g_yield','g_yieldmass'],need:5},`Atom economy and yield. Get 5 right to master it.`,`High atom economy and high yield are different things.`),
Q(`A reaction has 100% atom economy but a 40% yield. What does that mean?`,[`No waste products in theory, but much less product is actually made`,'It\'s perfectly efficient, and everything made is the product','40% of what it makes is waste, and 60% is the product','It can\'t happen, because the two numbers must be the same'],0,`Atom economy is about the equation; yield is about what really happens.`),
T(`<h3>Key points</h3><ul><li>Atom economy: from the equation. Yield: from the experiment.</li><li>Choose a route by weighing yield, atom economy, rate, cost, energy and sustainability.</li></ul>`)])
]});
U({id:'gc5-2',track:'gc',level:5,title:'Equilibrium and electrolysis',icon:'⇌',color:'#378ADD',desc:'Predicting equilibrium shifts in unfamiliar reactions, and writing half equations.',lessons:[
LH('gc5-2-1','Predicting equilibrium shifts',11,'Applying Le Chatelier to reactions you\'ve never seen.',[
T(`<h3>The method</h3><ol><li>What changed? (concentration, temperature, pressure)</li><li>Which direction opposes that change?</li><li>So which way does the equilibrium shift, and what happens to the yield?</li></ol><p>For pressure, count only gas molecules on each side. A catalyst never changes the position, only how fast equilibrium is reached.</p>`),
Q(`2NO₂(g) ⇌ N₂O₄(g); forward reaction exothermic. NO₂ is brown, N₂O₄ colourless. The mixture is cooled. What happens?`,[`It gets paler: equilibrium shifts right, the exothermic direction`,'It gets darker: equilibrium shifts left, making more NO₂','No change: cooling only affects how fast it reacts','It gets darker: cooling always favours the left-hand side'],0,`Cooling favours the exothermic direction.`),
Q(`H₂(g) + I₂(g) ⇌ 2HI(g). Pressure is increased. What happens to the yield of HI?`,[`No change: 2 gas molecules on each side`,'It increases: higher pressure always favours products','It decreases: equilibrium moves to the left side','It doubles: there are twice as many HI molecules'],0,`Equal numbers of gas molecules.`),
Q(`CO(g) + 2H₂(g) ⇌ CH₃OH(g), forward exothermic. Which conditions give the highest yield of methanol?`,[`Low temperature and high pressure`,`High temperature and high pressure`,`Low temperature and low pressure`,`High temperature and low pressure`],0,`Exothermic forward: low T. 3 molecules → 1: high P.`),
Q(`Adding a catalyst to an equilibrium mixture…`,[`doesn't change the position, but equilibrium is reached faster`,'increases the yield, because it speeds up the forward reaction','decreases the yield, because it speeds up the reverse reaction','moves the position right, but equilibrium takes longer to reach'],0,`It speeds up both directions equally.`),
T(`<h3>Key points</h3><ul><li>Equilibrium shifts to oppose the change.</li><li>Count gas molecules only for pressure.</li><li>Catalysts don't move the equilibrium.</li></ul>`)]),
LH('gc5-2-2','Half equations',11,'Writing and balancing half equations for electrolysis and redox.',[
RM(`<p>Cathode: reduction (gain of electrons). Anode: oxidation (loss of electrons). OIL RIG.</p>`),
T(`<h3>Writing half equations</h3><ul><li>Cathode (metal or hydrogen): <b>Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Cu</b>; <b>2H⁺ + 2e⁻ → H₂</b>.</li><li>Anode (halogen): <b>2Cl⁻ → Cl₂ + 2e⁻</b>.</li><li>Anode (oxygen): <b>4OH⁻ → O₂ + 2H₂O + 4e⁻</b>.</li></ul><p>Check that both atoms and charges balance on each side.</p>`),
Q(`Which half equation happens at the cathode when molten aluminium oxide is electrolysed?`,[`Al³⁺ + 3e⁻ → Al`,`Al → Al³⁺ + 3e⁻`,`2O²⁻ → O₂ + 4e⁻`,`Al³⁺ → Al + 3e⁻`],0,`Positive ions gain electrons at the cathode.`),
Q(`Complete: 2Br⁻ → Br₂ + ___`,[`2e⁻`,`e⁻`,`2e⁺`,`Br⁻`],0,`Two negative charges on the left, so 2 electrons on the right.`),
Q(`Fe + Cu²⁺ → Fe²⁺ + Cu. Which species is oxidised?`,[`Fe`,`Cu²⁺`,`Fe²⁺`,`Cu`],0,`Iron loses 2 electrons.`),
T(`<h3>Key points</h3><ul><li>Cathode: ions gain electrons. Anode: ions lose electrons.</li><li>Balance atoms and charge.</li><li>4OH⁻ → O₂ + 2H₂O + 4e⁻ for oxygen at the anode.</li></ul>`)])
]});
U({id:'gc5-3',track:'gc',level:5,title:'Unfamiliar contexts',icon:'?',color:'#1D9E75',desc:'Predicting properties from trends, and interpreting unfamiliar data.',lessons:[
LH('gc5-3-1','Predicting from trends',10,'Using periodic table and homologous series trends to predict unfamiliar substances.',[
T(`<h3>Trends you can extend</h3><ul><li>Group 1 gets more reactive going down; group 7 gets less reactive; group 0 boiling points rise.</li><li>In a homologous series, boiling point rises and flammability falls as the chain gets longer.</li></ul>`),
Q(`Predict how rubidium (below potassium in group 1) reacts with water.`,[`Even more violently than potassium, making rubidium hydroxide and hydrogen`,'Less violently than lithium, because its atoms are much heavier','It doesn\'t react, because its outer electron is held too tightly','Gently, like magnesium, making rubidium oxide and bubbles of oxygen gas at the surface'],0,`Reactivity increases down group 1.`),
Q(`Astatine is below iodine in group 7. Predict its state at room temperature.`,[`Solid`,`Gas`,`Liquid`,`Plasma`],0,`Melting points increase down group 7; iodine is already a solid.`),
Q(`Would astatine displace iodine from potassium iodide?`,[`No, astatine is less reactive than iodine`,'Yes, astatine is more reactive than iodine','Only when hot, because heat makes it react','Yes, because astatine is the biggest halogen'],0,`Reactivity decreases down group 7.`),
Q(`Pentane (C₅H₁₂) boils at 36 °C and heptane (C₇H₁₆) at 98 °C. Estimate the boiling point of hexane (C₆H₁₄).`,[`About 69 °C`,`About 20 °C`,`About 120 °C`,`About 36 °C`],0,`Halfway between gives a sensible estimate (it's actually 69 °C).`),
T(`<h3>Key points</h3><ul><li>Extend the trend, and explain it with electrons or forces.</li><li>Estimates between known values are reasonable; say "about".</li></ul>`)]),
LH('gc5-3-2','Interpreting data',10,'Reading tables and graphs about reactions you\'ve never met.',[
RM(`<p>Rate = amount ÷ time. Exothermic: temperature rises. Moles = mass ÷ M<sub>r</sub>.</p>`),
T(`<h3>Strategy</h3><p>Read the axes or column headings and units first. Look for the pattern, then any anomaly. Use numbers from the data in your answer: "the rate doubled from 0.2 to 0.4 g/s when the temperature rose by 10 °C".</p>`),
Q(`Reaction times: 20 °C, 80 s; 30 °C, 40 s; 40 °C, 20 s. What's the pattern?`,[`Every 10 °C rise halves the time, so doubles the rate`,'Every 10 °C rise doubles the time, so halves the rate','There\'s no clear pattern, so more data is needed','The rate stays the same, only the time changes'],0,`Quote the numbers.`),
N(`Using that pattern, predict the time at 50 °C.`,10,0.001,'s',`Halve again: 20 → 10 s.`),
Q(`Fuel A releases 30 kJ/g and makes 3.0 g CO₂ per g; fuel B releases 45 kJ/g and makes 3.1 g CO₂ per g. Which is better for the environment per kJ?`,[`Fuel B: less CO₂ per kJ of energy`,'Fuel A: less CO₂ per gram of fuel burned','They\'re about the same: CO₂ is nearly equal','You can\'t tell without knowing their prices'],0,`A: 0.10 g CO₂ per kJ; B: about 0.069 g per kJ.`),
T(`<h3>Key points</h3><ul><li>Headings and units first.</li><li>Describe the pattern with numbers from the data.</li><li>Compare fairly: per kJ, per gram, per mole.</li></ul>`)])
]});
U({id:'gc5-4',track:'gc',level:5,title:'Full-marks technique',icon:'✓',color:'#D4537E',desc:'Command words in chemistry, and the precise wording that grade 9 answers need.',lessons:[
LH('gc5-4-1','Command words and precise wording',10,'What each command word wants, and the chemistry words that get marks.',[
W('matchPairs',{pairs:[['Describe (a test)','Give the method and the result you\'d see'],['Explain (a property)','Link structure, bonding and forces to the property'],['Compare','Similarities and differences, mentioning both'],['Evaluate','Weigh up advantages and disadvantages, then judge'],['Calculate','Show working, give units and sensible significant figures']]},`Match each command word to what it wants in chemistry.`,`"Describe a test" needs the result too.`),
Q(`"Describe the test for carbon dioxide." Which answer gets full marks?`,[`Bubble the gas through limewater; it turns milky (cloudy)`,'Use limewater, and look for a change','It turns limewater cloudy, so it\'s CO₂','Hold a lit splint in the gas; it goes out with a squeaky pop'],0,`Method and result.`),
Q(`Which word should replace "atoms" in "sodium chloride is made of atoms held by strong bonds"?`,[`Ions`,`Molecules`,`Electrons`,`Elements`],0,`It's ionic: Na⁺ and Cl⁻ ions.`),
T(`<h3>Key points</h3><ul><li>Tests: method + result.</li><li>Use the right particle word: atom, ion, molecule, electron.</li></ul>`)]),
LH('gc5-4-2','How marks are lost',10,'Common slips in chemistry answers.',[
RM(`<p>Simple molecules: weak intermolecular forces, strong covalent bonds.</p>`),
T(`<h3>The usual suspects</h3><ul><li>"Breaking covalent bonds" when water boils (it's intermolecular forces).</li><li>"Cloudy" vs "clear": a colourless solution can still be clear.</li><li>Unbalanced equations, or wrong state symbols.</li><li>Forgetting to divide cm³ by 1000.</li><li>Saying a catalyst "gives energy" (it lowers activation energy).</li><li>Saying a strong acid is "concentrated" (strength is about ionisation).</li></ul>`),
Q(`A student says "a catalyst speeds up a reaction by giving particles more energy". Correct it.`,[`It provides a pathway with a lower activation energy`,'It heats the mixture, so the particles move faster','It adds more particles, so there are more collisions','It increases the concentration of the reactants'],0,`Particles have the same energy; more of them now have enough.`),
Q(`Which is the correct description of a dilute strong acid?`,[`Fully ionised, but with a low concentration of acid`,'Partly ionised, with a low concentration of acid','Fully ionised, with a high concentration of acid','Not ionised at all, with a lot of water added'],0,`Strength and concentration are different.`),
Q(`"Clear" and "colourless" mean…`,[`different things: clear means see-through, colourless means no colour`,'the same thing: both mean you can see through it','different things: clear means no colour, colourless means see-through','the same thing: both mean it has a pale white colour'],0,`Copper sulfate solution is clear but blue.`),
T(`<h3>Key points</h3><ul><li>Boiling simple molecules: intermolecular forces.</li><li>Catalyst: lower activation energy.</li><li>Strength ≠ concentration. Clear ≠ colourless.</li></ul>`)])
]});

U({id:'gm5-1',track:'gm',level:5,title:'Proof',icon:'∎',color:'#7F77DD',desc:'Algebraic proof with odd, even and consecutive numbers, and geometric proof using congruence and circle theorems.',lessons:[
LH('gm5-1-1','Algebraic proof',11,'Writing numbers with algebra so you can prove things for every case.',[
T(`<h3>The building blocks</h3><ul><li>Any integer: <b>n</b>. Consecutive integers: n, n + 1, n + 2.</li><li>Even: <b>2n</b>. Odd: <b>2n + 1</b>. Consecutive odd numbers: 2n + 1, 2n + 3.</li><li>A multiple of k: <b>k × (an integer)</b>. Take k out as a factor to show it.</li></ul><p>Checking a few examples isn't a proof. Algebra proves it for every number.</p>`),
W('stepCalc',{steps:[`Prove that the sum of any three consecutive integers is a multiple of 3.`,`Let the integers be n, n + 1 and n + 2`,`Sum = n + (n + 1) + (n + 2) = 3n + 3`,`= 3(n + 1)`,`3 × an integer, so it's always a multiple of 3 ∎`]},`A proof, step by step.`,`Factorise to show the multiple.`),
W('stepCalc',{steps:[`Prove that (2n + 1)² − (2n − 1)² is a multiple of 8 for any integer n.`,`(2n + 1)² = 4n² + 4n + 1`,`(2n − 1)² = 4n² − 4n + 1`,`Subtract: (4n² + 4n + 1) − (4n² − 4n + 1) = 8n`,`8n = 8 × n, a multiple of 8 ∎`]},`Another proof.`,`Expand, simplify, then factor out the number.`),
Q(`How should you write "the product of two odd numbers"?`,[`(2a + 1)(2b + 1)`,`(2n + 1)(2n + 1)`,`2n × 2n`,`(n + 1)(n + 2)`],0,`Different letters: the two odd numbers needn't be the same.`),
Q(`(2a + 1)(2b + 1) = 4ab + 2a + 2b + 1. How does this prove the product is odd?`,[`It equals 2(2ab + a + b) + 1, which is one more than an even number`,'Because it has a + 1 at the end, and anything ending in 1 is odd','Because 4ab is even, and even numbers multiply to make odd ones','It doesn\'t: you would need to try every value of a and b'],0,`Show the form 2k + 1.`),
T(`<h3>Key points</h3><ul><li>Even 2n, odd 2n + 1, consecutive n, n + 1.</li><li>Use different letters for independent numbers.</li><li>Finish by factorising to show the result.</li></ul>`)]),
LH('gm5-1-2','Geometric proof',11,'Proving triangles congruent and proving circle facts, with a reason for every step.',[
RM(`<p>Congruence: SSS, SAS, ASA, RHS. Circle theorems: centre = 2 × circumference, and so on.</p>`),
T(`<h3>How to write a geometric proof</h3><p>Every line needs a <b>statement</b> and a <b>reason</b>. For congruence, show three matching facts and name the condition (SSS, SAS, ASA or RHS).</p>`),
O(`Prove that the angle at the centre is twice the angle at the circumference. Put the steps in order.`,[`Draw the radius from the centre O through the point C on the circumference to divide the angle`,`OA = OC and OB = OC (radii), so triangles OAC and OBC are isosceles`,`So the base angles in each triangle are equal: call them x and y`,`The exterior angles at O are 2x and 2y (exterior angle = sum of the two opposite interior angles)`,`So angle AOB = 2x + 2y = 2(x + y) = 2 × angle ACB ∎`],`Isosceles triangles from radii are the key move.`),
Q(`ABCD is a parallelogram. To prove triangles ABC and CDA are congruent, which reason works?`,[`SSS: AB = CD, BC = DA (opposite sides of a parallelogram), AC is shared`,'AAA: the three angles match (opposite angles of a parallelogram)','RHS: there\'s a right angle, the hypotenuse AC, and a side','They aren\'t congruent: one is a reflection of the other one'],0,`Opposite sides equal plus a common side.`),
T(`<h3>Key points</h3><ul><li>Statement + reason on every line.</li><li>Radii give isosceles triangles.</li><li>Name the congruence condition at the end.</li></ul>`)])
]});
U({id:'gm5-2',track:'gm',level:5,title:'Functions and graphs',icon:'ƒ',color:'#1D9E75',desc:'Graph transformations, the equation of a circle and tangents to circles.',lessons:[
LH('gm5-2-1','Graph transformations',10,'Translations and reflections of y = f(x).',[
T(`<h3>Four rules</h3><ul><li><b>y = f(x) + a</b>: up by a.</li><li><b>y = f(x + a)</b>: <b>left</b> by a (inside the bracket does the opposite).</li><li><b>y = −f(x)</b>: reflect in the x-axis.</li><li><b>y = f(−x)</b>: reflect in the y-axis.</li></ul>`),
W('fnPlot',{x:[-6,6],y:[-6,10],xl:'x',yl:'y',sl:[['a','Shift inside the bracket',-3,3,1,0,function(v){return 'f(x '+(v<0?'− '+(-v):'+ '+v)+')'}]],curves:function(p){return [[function(x){return x*x-2},'--muted',2,[6,4]],[function(x){return (x+p.a)*(x+p.a)-2},'--accent',3]]},read:function(p){return 'y = f(x '+(p.a<0?'− '+(-p.a):'+ '+p.a)+') moves the dashed graph '+(p.a===0?'nowhere':p.a>0?'<b>left</b> by '+p.a:'<b>right</b> by '+(-p.a))+'. The minimum moves from (0, −2) to ('+(-p.a)+', −2).'}},`Slide the shift and watch which way the graph moves.`,`f(x + a) moves left. It catches everyone out once.`),
W('calcDrill',{skill:'g_functrans',need:5},`Describe the transformations. Get 5 right to master it.`,`Inside the bracket: opposite direction, on x.`),
T(`<h3>Key points</h3><ul><li>Outside: acts on y as expected. Inside: acts on x, opposite way.</li><li>−f(x): flip in the x-axis. f(−x): flip in the y-axis.</li></ul>`)]),
LH('gm5-2-2','Circles and tangents',10,'x² + y² = r², and the equation of a tangent to a circle.',[
RM(`<p>Perpendicular gradients multiply to −1. A tangent meets the radius at 90°.</p>`),
T(`<h3>Equation of a circle</h3><p>A circle with centre (0, 0) and radius r: <b>x² + y² = r²</b>.</p><h3>Tangent at a point (a, b)</h3><ol><li>Gradient of the radius = b ÷ a.</li><li>Tangent gradient = −a ÷ b (perpendicular).</li><li>Use y − b = m(x − a) to get the equation.</li></ol>`),
W('stepCalc',{steps:[`Find the tangent to x² + y² = 25 at (3, 4).`,`Radius gradient = 4 ÷ 3`,`Tangent gradient = −3 ÷ 4`,`y − 4 = −¾(x − 3)`,`y = −¾x + 9/4 + 4, so <b>y = −¾x + 25/4</b> (or 4y + 3x = 25)`]},`A worked example.`,`Radius gradient, flip and negate, then y − b = m(x − a).`),
W('calcDrill',{skill:'g_circeq',need:4},`Circle equations and tangents. Get 4 right to master it.`,`r = √(right-hand side).`),
T(`<h3>Key points</h3><ul><li>x² + y² = r²: centre (0, 0), radius r.</li><li>Tangent gradient = −1 ÷ radius gradient.</li><li>y − b = m(x − a).</li></ul>`)])
]});
U({id:'gm5-3',track:'gm',level:5,title:'Hardest geometry',icon:'△',color:'#D85A30',desc:'Vector proof, segments, 3D trigonometry and the hardest sine and cosine rule problems.',lessons:[
LH('gm5-3-1','Vector proof',11,'Proving lines are parallel or points are collinear using vectors.',[
T(`<h3>Key ideas</h3><ul><li>Parallel: one vector is a multiple of the other.</li><li><b>Collinear</b> (on one straight line): AB and BC are parallel <b>and</b> share point B.</li><li>A point dividing AB in the ratio m : n is m/(m + n) of the way along: AP = m/(m + n) × AB.</li></ul>`),
W('stepCalc',{steps:[`OA = a, OB = b. M is the midpoint of AB. Find OM.`,`AB = b − a`,`AM = ½AB = ½(b − a)`,`OM = OA + AM = a + ½b − ½a`,`<b>OM = ½a + ½b</b> = ½(a + b)`]},`A worked example.`,`Route: from O to A, then along to M.`),
Q(`AB = 2a + 6b and BC = a + 3b. What can you conclude?`,[`A, B and C are collinear`,`AB is perpendicular to BC`,`AB = BC`,`Nothing`],0,`AB = 2BC, so they're parallel, and B is shared.`),
Q(`P divides AB in the ratio 1 : 3. What is AP?`,[`¼AB`,`⅓AB`,`¾AB`,`3AB`],0,`1 part out of 4.`),
T(`<h3>Key points</h3><ul><li>Multiple → parallel. Parallel + shared point → collinear.</li><li>Ratio m : n → m/(m + n) of the way.</li></ul>`)]),
LH('gm5-3-2','Segments, 3D and harder trig',11,'Combining sectors, triangles, 3D shapes and the cosine rule.',[
RM(`<p>Segment = sector − triangle. 3D: d = √(a² + b² + c²). Cosine rule for two sides and the included angle.</p>`),
W('calcDrill',{skill:['g_segment','g_pythag3d','g_cosrule','g_sinerule','g_areasine'],need:8},`Hardest geometry mix. Get 8 right to master it.`,`Draw the triangle you need, label it, then pick the rule.`),
Q(`The sine rule gives sin B = 0.8. Why might there be two possible angles?`,[`sin 53.1° and sin 126.9° are both 0.8`,'Calculators round sin B, which gives two answers','The triangle is right-angled, so B could be 90°','There aren\'t: inverse sine only ever gives one angle'],0,`The ambiguous case: check which fits the triangle.`),
T(`<h3>Key points</h3><ul><li>Sketch the triangle you're working in.</li><li>Sine rule angles can be ambiguous: 180° − θ is also possible.</li></ul>`)])
]});
U({id:'gm5-4',track:'gm',level:5,title:'Hardest problems',icon:'★',color:'#BA7517',desc:'Forming and solving equations from worded problems, and the grade 9 mixed drill.',lessons:[
LH('gm5-4-1','Algebraic problem solving',11,'Turning words into equations, especially quadratics, then solving and checking.',[
T(`<h3>The method</h3><ol><li>Choose a letter for the unknown and write what it means.</li><li>Turn each sentence into algebra.</li><li>Form an equation, rearrange, solve.</li><li>Check the answer makes sense (lengths can't be negative).</li></ol>`),
W('stepCalc',{steps:[`A rectangle's length is 3 cm more than its width. Its area is 40 cm². Find the width.`,`Let the width be x, so the length is x + 3`,`x(x + 3) = 40 → x² + 3x − 40 = 0`,`(x + 8)(x − 5) = 0 → x = −8 or x = 5`,`A width can't be negative, so <b>x = 5 cm</b>`]},`A worked example.`,`Reject answers that don't fit the context.`),
Q(`The sum of a number and its reciprocal is 2.5. Which equation fits?`,[`x + 1/x = 2.5, so 2x² − 5x + 2 = 0`,'x² + 1 = 2.5, so x² − 1.5 = 0','x + 1/x = 2.5, so x² − 2.5 = 0','x − 1/x = 2.5, so 2x² − 5x − 2 = 0'],0,`Multiply by 2x: 2x² + 2 = 5x.`),
N(`A bag has n counters, 4 of them red. Two are taken without replacement. P(both red) = 2/15. Find n.`,10,0.001,'',`4/n × 3/(n − 1) = 2/15 → 12 × 15 = 2n(n − 1) → n² − n − 90 = 0 → (n − 10)(n + 9) = 0, so n = 10.`),
T(`<h3>Key points</h3><ul><li>Define your letter, translate, form, solve, check.</li><li>Reject negative lengths and non-integer counts.</li></ul>`)]),
LH('gm5-4-2','Grade 9 mixed',12,'Everything from the top end of the Higher paper, mixed together.',[
RM(`<p>Read carefully, plan, show working, and check your answers fit the question.</p>`),
W('calcDrill',{skill:GM9,need:10},`The grade 9 drill. Get 10 right to master it.`,`If one stumps you, read the explanation, then try the next.`),
T(`<h3>Key points</h3><ul><li>Grade 9 is about combining topics confidently.</li><li>Keep practising mixed questions until they feel routine.</li></ul>`)])
]});

